\title{Exact Singleton Defect and the Asymptotic Capacity of Quantum Codes}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{22 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For quantum-code parameters $[[n,k,d]]$ satisfying the quantum Singleton inequality $k+2(d-1)\le n$ and $d>0$, we isolate the exact finite-length capacity budget $D=n+2-2d$. We call $D$ the exact Singleton defect and distinguish it from the geometric defect $G=n-2d$. The Singleton inequality yields $0\le k\le D$, while $D=G+2$. Every protected entropy $S$ bounded above by $k$ therefore satisfies $S\le D$. The logical rate $R=k/n$ is bounded by $D/n$, and the normalized exact defect differs from $G/n$ by precisely $2/n$. For families with diverging block length this endpoint correction vanishes. If $G$ is uniformly bounded above, both logical rate and every nonnegative protected-entropy density $S/n$ tend to zero. Conversely, rate at least $\varepsilon$ forces normalized exact defect at least $\varepsilon$. These conclusions require no locality or geometric hypothesis beyond Singleton. They constitute a necessary capacity law, not a sufficient construction theorem.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A quantum error-correcting code distributes logical quantum information over a larger physical system. Its parameters $[[n,k,d]]$ record the number $n$ of physical qubits, the number $k$ of logical qubits, and the distance $d$. Increasing $k$ improves storage efficiency, while increasing $d$ improves resilience; both consume the finite resource $n$.

The quantum Singleton inequality captures this competition:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:singleton}
k+2(d-1)\le n.
\end{equation}
Rearrangement gives $k\le n+2-2d$. The right side is the exact parameter-level capacity not consumed by distance. We denote it by $D$. The geometrically natural comparison $G=n-2d$ differs from it by a universal constant, $D=G+2$.

This distinction is important. At finite length, $D$ rather than $G$ is the nonnegative upper budget. In particular, $G$ may be negative, whereas the Singleton assumptions force $D\ge0$. At large block length, their normalized forms differ only by $2/n$. Thus $D$ is the correct finite budget and $G$ an asymptotically equivalent geometric proxy.

For one code we prove $k\le D$ and $S\le D$ for every protected entropy $S\le k$. For a family with $G\le B$, one has $k\le B+2$ uniformly. If block length diverges, logical rate and every nonnegative protected-entropy density tend to zero. Conversely, rate at least $\varepsilon$ forces $D/n\ge\varepsilon$.

These are parameter-level obstructions. They do not assert that available defect can always be converted into logical information. Such a converse needs additional locality, geometry, expansion, incidence, or decoding structure.

\section{Definitions and setting}

\begin{definition}[Admissible parameters]
An admissible parameter triple is a triple of nonnegative integers $(n,k,d)$ with $d>0$ satisfying \eqref{eq:singleton}. A code family is a sequence $(n_i,k_i,d_i)_{i\ge0}$ of admissible triples. Its block length diverges when $n_i\to\infty$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Exact and geometric defects]
For an admissible triple, its exact Singleton defect and geometric defect are, respectively,
\[
D=n+2-2d,\qquad G=n-2d.
\]
The word ``geometric'' describes the direct balance between length and twice the distance; no geometric realization is assumed.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Endpoint relation]\label{lem:endpoint}
The defects satisfy $D=G+2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Direct substitution gives $(n-2d)+2=n+2-2d$.
\end{proof}

For $n>0$, define the logical rate and normalized exact defect by
\[
R=\frac{k}{n},\qquad \delta=\frac{D}{n}.
\]
The geometric defect density is $G/n$. A protected entropy is any real quantity $S$ satisfying $S\le k$. Density results will additionally assume $S\ge0$. This abstracts any operational information quantity, measured in logical-qubit units, that cannot exceed the logical dimension.

\section{Finite-length capacity laws}

\begin{theorem}[Exact finite-length defect-capacity law]\label{thm:capacity}
Every admissible parameter triple satisfies
\[
0\le k\le D.
\]
In particular, $D\ge0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Because $d>0$, integer arithmetic rewrites \eqref{eq:singleton} as
\[
k+2d\le n+2.
\]
Subtracting $2d$ gives $k\le n+2-2d=D$. Since $k$ is nonnegative, $0\le k\le D$, hence $D\ge0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Protected-entropy bound]\label{cor:entropy}
If $S\le k$, then $S\le D$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Combine $S\le k$ with Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity} identifies $D$, not $G$, as the exact finite-size budget. If $n=2d$, then $G=0$ but $D=2$, so at most two logical qubits remain possible. Exact balance does not eliminate all logical information; it limits that information to a scale-independent constant.

\begin{theorem}[Uniform protected-information bound]\label{thm:uniform}
Let $(n_i,k_i,d_i)$ be a code family and let $S_i\le k_i$. If an integer $B$ satisfies $G_i\le B$ for every $i$, then
\[
k_i\le B+2,\qquad S_i\le B+2
\]
for every $i$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:endpoint} gives $D_i=G_i+2\le B+2$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity} and Corollary~\ref{cor:entropy}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rate-defect inequality]\label{thm:rate}
For every admissible triple with $n>0$,
\[
R\le\delta,
\quad\text{that is,}\quad
\frac{k}{n}\le\frac{n+2-2d}{n}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Divide $k\le D$ by the positive number $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Normalized endpoint identity]\label{thm:normendpoint}
For every admissible triple with $n>0$,
\[
\delta=\frac{G}{n}+\frac{2}{n}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Divide $D=G+2$ by $n$ and distribute division over addition.
\end{proof}

Consequently,
\[
0\le R\le \frac{G}{n}+\frac{2}{n}.
\]
Although $G/n$ alone may be negative in a small example, the full right side is nonnegative for admissible data.

\section{Asymptotic consequences}

\begin{theorem}[Asymptotic equivalence of defects]\label{thm:equiv}
If a code family has $n_i\to\infty$, then
\[
\frac{D_i}{n_i}-\frac{G_i}{n_i}\longrightarrow0.
\]
The difference is exactly $2/n_i$ at every index.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:normendpoint}, the difference equals $2/n_i$. Since $n_i\to\infty$, this tends to zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bounded-defect zero-rate theorem]\label{thm:zero-rate}
Let $(n_i,k_i,d_i)$ be a code family with $n_i\to\infty$. If $G_i\le B$ for all $i$, then
\[
\frac{k_i}{n_i}\longrightarrow0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The logical rate is nonnegative, and Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform} yields
\[
0\le\frac{k_i}{n_i}\le\frac{B+2}{n_i}.
\]
The upper bound tends to zero. The squeeze theorem proves the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No extensive information at exact balance]
If $n_i=2d_i+C_i$, where the integers $C_i$ are uniformly bounded above and $n_i\to\infty$, then $k_i/n_i\to0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Here $G_i=C_i$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:zero-rate} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bounded-defect zero-entropy-density theorem]\label{thm:zero-entropy}
Let $(n_i,k_i,d_i)$ be a code family with $n_i>0$ and $n_i\to\infty$. Suppose $0\le S_i\le k_i$. If $G_i\le B$ for every $i$, then
\[
\frac{S_i}{n_i}\longrightarrow0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform}, $S_i\le B+2$. Hence
\[
0\le\frac{S_i}{n_i}\le\frac{B+2}{n_i}.
\]
The squeeze theorem completes the proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative necessary defect density]\label{thm:positive}
For an admissible triple with $n>0$, if $\varepsilon\le R$, then $\varepsilon\le\delta$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:rate} gives $R\le\delta$, so $\varepsilon\le R\le\delta$.
\end{proof}

For a family whose rates are bounded below by a positive $\varepsilon$, each normalized exact defect is at least $\varepsilon$. Theorem~\ref{thm:equiv} then transfers the asymptotic necessity to geometric defect density. The converse does not follow: a large capacity budget may remain unused.

\section{Algorithms and numerical diagnostics}

For one triple, verify $n,k\ge0$, $d>0$, and \eqref{eq:singleton}. Compute $D=n+2-2d$ and $G=n-2d$. Check $D=G+2$ and $0\le k\le D$. When $n>0$, compute $R=k/n$ and $\delta=D/n$. This takes a constant number of arithmetic operations, hence $O(1)$ arithmetic-operation complexity. For binary integers of bit length $L$, additions, comparisons, and multiplication by $2$ have $O(L)$ bit cost.

For $m$ triples, scan the data row by row. Given a proposed bound $B$, verify $G_i\le B$ and report
\[
k_i\le B+2,\qquad
R_i\le\frac{B+2}{n_i},\qquad
\frac{S_i}{n_i}\le\frac{B+2}{n_i}.
\]
The scan uses $O(m)$ arithmetic operations and $O(1)$ auxiliary memory if streamed, or $O(m)$ memory if all reports are retained.

Three synthetic parameter families illustrate the regimes. First, for
\[
n_i=2i,\quad d_i=i,\quad k_i=2,
\]
one has $G_i=0$, $D_i=2$, and $R_i=1/i\to0$. Second, for
\[
n_i=2i+4,\quad d_i=i,\quad k_i=6,
\]
one has $G_i=4$, $D_i=6$, and $R_i=6/(2i+4)\to0$. Third, for
\[
n_i=4i,\quad d_i=i,\quad k_i=2i+2,
\]
one has $G_i=2i$, $D_i=2i+2$, and
\[
R_i=\frac{2i+2}{4i}\longrightarrow\frac12.
\]
All three saturate the Singleton inequality at the parameter level. These examples demonstrate arithmetic compatibility, not existence of a realized quantum code for every listed triple.

\section{Interpretation and applications}

\subsection{Capacity budget versus realizable capacity}

The exact defect is a ceiling. Small $D/n$ forces small rate; large $D/n$ merely permits a large rate. Equality $k=D$ means saturation of Singleton and is not universal. Any lower rate bound must use information beyond $(n,k,d)$.

\subsection{Geometrically local families}

For local stabilizer or subsystem codes on lattices, graphs, cell complexes, or manifolds, $G=n-2d$ is a natural global observable. Theorems~\ref{thm:zero-rate} and \ref{thm:zero-entropy} apply without using geometry: bounded $G$ rules out extensive protected information. Geometry enters only when seeking a converse. Expansion, bounded degree, local testability, dimensional constraints, or homological structure might force some available defect to support logical degrees of freedom.

\subsection{Cuts, entropy, and syndrome geometry}

A global defect cannot describe entanglement for every boundary region. Tensor-network settings motivate cut-indexed defects associated with regional erasure thresholds or minimal cuts. An entropy-area identity would require both a cut-wise coding inequality and a separate saturation theorem.

Likewise, two codes may share $[[n,k,d]]$, and hence $D$, $G$, $R$, and $\delta$, while having different stabilizer generators, syndrome graphs, decoder moves, or locality. Parameter arithmetic constrains information capacity but cannot reconstruct incidence geometry or a bulk metric.

\section{Further deductions and design principles}

The defect law gives an explicit finite-size threshold for a target rate. If $r\le k/n$, then Theorem~\ref{thm:positive} implies
\[
D\ge rn,\qquad G\ge rn-2.
\]
A fixed positive target rate therefore requires geometric defect growing linearly with block length, up to the endpoint correction. Merely unbounded growth such as $\sqrt n$ or $\log n$ remains sublinear and cannot support a rate bounded below by a positive constant under Singleton.

The arithmetic can also be solved for distance:
\[
d\le \frac{n-k+2}{2},
\qquad
d\le \left\lfloor\frac{n-k+2}{2}\right\rfloor
\]
because $d$ is integral. Thus fixing physical size and logical payload imposes an explicit distance ceiling.

Finally, define the Singleton slack
\[
\sigma=D-k.
\]
Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity} gives $\sigma\ge0$, with $\sigma=0$ precisely at Singleton saturation. Normalization yields
\[
\delta-R=\frac{\sigma}{n}.
\]
This separates two causes of low rate: defect density itself may be small, or a construction may leave a large part of its available defect unused. If $\delta_i$ has a positive limit while $R_i\to0$, normalized slack approaches that positive limit. Conversely, if $\sigma_i/n_i\to0$, then rate and exact-defect density have the same asymptotic behavior. Explaining the slack is a structural task for geometry and code architecture beyond the universal inequality.

\section{Scope and limitations}

The assumptions are only nonnegative integer parameters, positive distance, and Singleton. No claim is made that every admissible triple is realized by a quantum code. The protected entropy is abstract; applications must justify $S\le k$, and density applications also $S\ge0$.

Uniform boundedness of $G$ is sufficient but not conceptually maximal. If $G_i=o(n_i)$, then
\[
0\le\frac{k_i}{n_i}\le\frac{G_i}{n_i}+\frac{2}{n_i}
\]
suggests the same zero-rate conclusion, with an identical extension for protected entropy. Most importantly, no lower rate bound follows from positive defect density alone.

\section{Future research directions}

A first target is a concrete class of local CSS codes on bounded-degree cell complexes, with expansion or local-testability hypotheses strong enough to convert defect density into a rate lower bound. A second is a cut-wise Singleton inequality for finite tensor networks, followed by a distinct study of entropy saturation. A third is the construction of parameter-matched codes with inequivalent syndrome adjacency graphs, clarifying the geometric information absent from $[[n,k,d]]$. A fourth is a canonical local defect field for subsystem codes, robust under choices in allocating checks and logical degrees of freedom. Finally, the bounded-defect theorems should be generalized explicitly to sublinear defect $G_i=o(n_i)$.

\section{Conclusion}

The quantum Singleton inequality contains the exact capacity budget
\[
D=n+2-2d.
\]
Every admissible triple satisfies $0\le k\le D$, and every protected entropy bounded by $k$ is bounded by $D$. The geometric defect $G=n-2d$ differs by the universal endpoint correction $2$, so their normalized forms become asymptotically identical.

If $G$ is uniformly bounded while $n$ diverges, logical rate and every nonnegative protected-entropy density bounded by logical dimension tend to zero. Conversely, positive rate requires at least the same normalized exact defect. Parameter arithmetic therefore establishes defect as necessary capacity. Determining when geometric structure makes that capacity sufficient remains the central open direction.

\end{document}

