# Explore the Singleton Defect Capacity Law

A quantum code with parameters $[[n,k,d]]$ obeys the universal constraint $k+2(d-1)\le n$. This presentation turns that inequality into an intuitive capacity budget. For background, see the [Error Correction Zoo](https://errorcorrectionzoo.org/c/qecc) and the [quantum Singleton bound](https://errorcorrectionzoo.org/list/quantum_bounds).

## 1. Audit a finite code

Start by changing $n$, $k$, and $d$. The auditor computes the exact defect $D=n+2-2d$, the geometric defect $G=n-2d$, and verifies $k\le D$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the finite-length proof</summary>
The Singleton inequality rearranges as $k+2d\le n+2$, hence $k\le n+2-2d=D$. Since $k\ge0$, the exact defect is nonnegative. Also $D=(n-2d)+2=G+2$.
</details>

{{demo:0}}

## 2. Watch the two-qubit correction disappear

The exact and geometric densities differ by $2/n$. At finite size this correction matters; for growing block length it vanishes.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Why the limit is automatic</summary>
For every positive $n$, $D/n-G/n=2/n$. Therefore any sequence with $n\to\infty$ has difference tending to zero.
</details>

## 3. Bounded defect means zero density

If $G\le B$, then $k\le B+2$. Consequently both $k/n$ and every protected-entropy density $S/n$ with $0\le S\le k$ lie below $(B+2)/n$.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the squeeze argument</summary>
The inequalities $0\le k/n\le(B+2)/n$ and $0\le S/n\le(B+2)/n$ place both densities between zero and a quantity tending to zero. The squeeze theorem gives both limits.
</details>

## 4. Positive rate has a necessary cost

A claimed rate floor $\varepsilon\le k/n$ forces $\varepsilon\le D/n$. This is necessary, not sufficient: unused capacity can remain.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Boundary of the conclusion</summary>
The arithmetic supplies no reverse inequality. Locality, expansion, check geometry, or another structural hypothesis would be needed to infer positive rate from positive defect density.
</details>

